3) Implementation of REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING (auto-add @story via require-story-annotation):
- Rule module explicitly tied to story 008 and requirement IDs:
  src/rules/require-story-annotation.ts
  ```ts
  /**
   * ESLint rule module: require-story-annotation
   * @story docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md
   * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
   * @req REQ-ANNOTATION-REQUIRED
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING - This rule supports auto-fixing missing @story annotations per Story 008.0 auto-fix behavior.
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE - Auto-fix behavior only inserts @story annotation JSDoc comments and never changes executable or runtime code.
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE - Auto-fix inserts a minimal placeholder JSDoc in a way that preserves existing surrounding formatting and structure.
   */
  ...
  const rule: Rule.RuleModule = {
    meta: {
      type: "problem",
      docs: {
        description:
          "Require @story annotations on functions and auto-fix missing annotations where possible",
        recommended: "error",
      },
      hasSuggestions: true,
      /**
       * Auto-fix support for inserting @story annotations.
       * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
       * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING - `fixable: \"code\"` is used to implement REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING for missing @story annotations.
       * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE ...
       * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE ...
       */
      fixable: "code",
      messages: {
        missingStory:
          "Function '{{name}}' must have an explicit @story annotation. Add a JSDoc or line comment with @story that points to the implementing story file ...",
      },
      ...
    },
    create(context) {
      const sourceCode = context.getSourceCode();
      const opts = (context.options && context.options[0]) || {};
      const scope = opts.scope || DEFAULT_SCOPE;
      const exportPriority = opts.exportPriority || "all";
      const should = (node: any) => shouldProcessNode(node, scope, exportPriority);
      return buildVisitors(context, sourceCode, {
        shouldProcessNode: should,
        scope,
        exportPriority,
      });
    },
  };
  ```

- Core auto-fix helpers insert a fixed template annotation immediately before the target without altering code:
  src/rules/helpers/require-story-helpers.ts
  ```ts
  const STORY_PATH = "docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md";
  const ANNOTATION = `/** @story ${STORY_PATH} */`;
  ```

  src/rules/helpers/require-story-core.ts
  ```ts
  export function createAddStoryFix(target: any) {
    function addStoryFixer(fixer: any) {
      const start = ...; // chooses parent export range or node range start
      return fixer.insertTextBeforeRange([start, start], `${ANNOTATION}\n`);
    }
    return addStoryFixer;
  }

  export function createMethodFix(node: any) {
    function methodFixer(fixer: any) {
      const start = ...; // similar logic for methods
      return fixer.insertTextBeforeRange([start, start], `${ANNOTATION}\n  `);
    }
    return methodFixer;
  }
  ```

- The reporting helpers wire in these fixers and suggestions, satisfying the behavior described in the story:
  src/rules/helpers/require-story-helpers.ts (newer helper-based implementation)
  ```ts
  function reportMissing(
    context: Rule.RuleContext,
    sourceCode: any,
    node: any,
    passedTarget?: any,
  ): void {
    const functionName = extractName(...);
    if (hasStoryAnnotation(sourceCode, node)) {
      return; // do not report if already annotated
    }
    const resolvedTarget = passedTarget ?? resolveTargetNode(sourceCode, node);
    const name = functionName;
    const nameNode = ...;

    context.report({
      node: nameNode,
      messageId: "missingStory",
      data: { name, functionName: name },
      fix: createAddStoryFix(resolvedTarget),
      suggest: [
        {
          desc: `Add JSDoc @story annotation for function '${name}', e.g., ${ANNOTATION}`,
          fix: createAddStoryFix(resolvedTarget),
        },
      ],
    });
  }
  ```

- The test suite demonstrates this actually adds the placeholder annotation for multiple node types (FunctionDeclaration, FunctionExpression, class method, TS declare function, TS method signature) and leaves already-annotated nodes unchanged.

4) Implementation of REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT, REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE, REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE (valid-annotation-format suffix normalization):
- Rule file ties to Story 008 and requirements:
  src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts
  ```ts
  import { collapseAnnotationValue, TAG_NOT_FOUND_INDEX, getFixedStoryPath, ... } from "./helpers/valid-annotation-utils";
  ...
  /**
   * Compute the text replacement for an invalid @story annotation within a comment.
   * ... Returns null when the tag or value cannot be safely located.
   * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE
   */
  function createStoryFix(context: any, comment: any, fixed: string): null | (() => any) {
    const sourceCode = context.getSourceCode();
    const commentText = sourceCode.getText(comment);
    const search = "@story";
    const tagIndex = commentText.indexOf(search);
    if (tagIndex === TAG_NOT_FOUND_INDEX) {
      return null;
    }
    ... // compute exact value substring range
    const fixRange: [number, number] = [ start + valueStartInComment, start + valueEndInComment ];
    return () => (fixer: any) => fixer.replaceTextRange(fixRange, fixed);
  }

  /**
   * Report an invalid @story annotation and attempt a minimal, safe auto-fix
   * for common path suffix issues ...
   * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
   * @req REQ-PATH-FORMAT
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE
   * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE
   */
  function reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix(
    context: any,
    comment: any,
    collapsed: string,
    fixed: string,
  ): void {
    const fixFactory = createStoryFix(context, comment, fixed);
    if (!fixFactory) {
      // if we cannot safely locate the value, report without a fix
      reportInvalidStoryFormat(context, comment, collapsed, getResolvedDefaults());
      return;
    }

    context.report({
      node: comment as any,
      messageId: "invalidStoryFormat",
      data: { details: buildStoryErrorMessage("invalid", collapsed, getResolvedDefaults()) },
      fix: fixFactory(),
    });
  }

  function validateStoryAnnotation(...): void {
    const trimmed = rawValue.trim();
    ...
    const collapsed = collapseAnnotationValue(trimmed);
    const pathPattern = options.storyPattern;

    if (pathPattern.test(collapsed)) {
      return; // already valid
    }

    if (/\s/.test(trimmed)) {
      // values with whitespace are treated as complex -> no auto-fix
      reportInvalidStoryFormat(context, comment, collapsed, options);
      return;
    }

    const fixed = getFixedStoryPath(collapsed);
    if (fixed && pathPattern.test(fixed)) {
      // only when safe suffix fix produces a valid path do we apply auto-fix
      reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix(context, comment, collapsed, fixed);
      return;
    }

    // fall back to plain error, no fix
    reportInvalidStoryFormat(context, comment, collapsed, options);
  }

  export default {
    meta: {
      ...
      /**
       * This rule's fixable support is limited to safe @story path suffix normalization per Story 008.0.
       * Fixes are limited strictly to adjusting the suffix portion of the @story path ...
       * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
       * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE
       * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE
       */
      fixable: "code",
    },
    create(context: any) { ... }
  } as any;
  ```

- This logic ensures:
  * Only simple, suffix-only transformations from `getFixedStoryPath` are applied.
  * When no safe transformation exists (whitespace/complex paths, or `getFixedStoryPath` returns null or still invalid), the rule only reports an error without a fix.
  * The fix operates on the minimal `value` substring inside the comment via `replaceTextRange`, preserving all other whitespace and text.

- The dedicated auto-fix tests in tests/rules/auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts validate both the “already correct – no change” case and the two suffix-fix scenarios (adding `.md` to `.story` and adding `.story.md` when there's no extension), matching the story’s REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT description.

5) Acceptance criteria: Core functionality, quality, integration, UX, error handling, documentation
- Core Functionality (ESLint --fix resolves common annotation violations):
  * require-story-annotation has `fixable: "code"` and uses `createAddStoryFix` / `createMethodFix` to insert @story lines when missing.
  * valid-annotation-format has `fixable: "code"` and uses `getFixedStoryPath` + `reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix` to normalize `.story`/`.md` suffixes.
  * Dedicated tests (auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts) and the passing Jest run confirm these behaviors work as specified.

- Quality Standards (safe, preserve functionality/formatting):
  * require-story-annotation only adds comments before existing code; no changes to function bodies or runtime expressions.
  * valid-annotation-format only replaces the exact @story path value substring; all comment structure and surrounding whitespace remain intact.
  * Story text explicitly limits scope to these safe scenarios; code and tests match that scope.

- Integration with ESLint toolchain:
  * Both rules expose standard ESLint RuleModule structures with meta.docs, meta.fixable, and create(context) functions.
  * They are exercised by `RuleTester` in tests, verifying correct behavior within ESLint’s execution model.

- User Experience (consistent, predictable fixes):
  * require-story-annotation covers multiple function-like patterns: JS function declarations, function expressions, class methods, TS declare functions, TS method signatures (as proven by tests with different node kinds).
  * valid-annotation-format only auto-fixes when paths fit the simple suffix patterns, preventing surprising changes.
  * Suggestion descriptions in require-story-helpers.ts give clear guidance on what is being added.

- Error Handling (graceful when fix cannot be applied):
  * valid-annotation-format: if the value cannot be safely located (createStoryFix returns null) or if getFixedStoryPath cannot produce a valid match, it calls `reportInvalidStoryFormat` with no fix, satisfying “report without attempting a fix”.
  * require-story-annotation helpers wrap detection and reporting in try/catch blocks and check `hasStoryAnnotation` first, preventing spurious or unsafe fixes.

- Documentation (auto-fix capabilities documented with examples):
  * user-docs/api-reference.md – traceability/require-story-annotation section:
    ```md
    Description: ... When run with `--fix`, the rule inserts a single-line placeholder JSDoc `@story` annotation above missing functions, methods, TypeScript declare functions, and interface method signatures using a built-in template aligned with Story 008.0. This template is currently fixed but structured for future configurability, and fixes are strictly limited to adding this placeholder annotation without altering the function body or changing any runtime behavior.
    ```
  * user-docs/api-reference.md – traceability/valid-annotation-format section:
    ```md
    Description: ... When run with `--fix`, the rule limits changes to safe `@story` path suffix normalization only—for example, adding `.md` when the path ends with `.story`, or adding `.story.md` when the base path has no extension—using targeted replacements implemented in the `getFixedStoryPath` and `reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix` helpers. It does not change directories, infer new story names, or modify any surrounding comment text or whitespace, in line with Story 008.0; more advanced path normalization strategies and selective toggles to enable or disable specific auto-fix behaviors are not yet implemented.
    ```
  * These docs directly match the implemented behavior and the tests.

6) Requirements marked as “not yet implemented” in the story are explicitly out-of-scope for this iteration and are correctly *not* present in the code:
- REQ-AUTOFIX-TEMPLATE (configurable templates) and REQ-AUTOFIX-SELECTIVE (fine-grained toggling of fix types) are documented in the story as "Not yet implemented". The current implementation uses a fixed template (STORY_PATH constant) and does not expose additional configuration flags beyond standard ESLint rule enabling/disabling, which is consistent with the story’s own description of the current iteration.

Given:
- All implemented requirements for this story (REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING, REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT, REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE, REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE) are clearly implemented in code with traceability annotations.
- Dedicated tests for this story and its requirements exist and pass.
- Documentation describing the auto-fix behavior is present and accurate.
- More advanced requirements explicitly flagged as future work are intentionally not implemented and are not part of the checked acceptance criteria.

The implementation of docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md is fully satisfied for the scope defined by this story.
